  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 AM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nLight rain showers/virga have been slowing expanding overnight,\nand the trend will just continue today. We will see an uptick of\nprecipitation amounts late this morning across the southeast\ncorner, and then most areas outside of the central mountains\nthis afternoon. We do expect thunderstorms today, and there is\nthe potential in addition to some decent downpours, to see some\nstronger winds. There is a 20-70% chance for gusts over 35 mph,\nperhaps exceeding 45...IF we can get some stronger storms to\nfire today. Otherwise, precipitation amounts quickly ramp up\nstarting tonight and continuing through Monday morning. There is\nstill some \"wobbling\" on where the heaviest amounts will fall,\nbut there is still a pretty wide swath of 1-2\" amounts across\ncentral and eastern Idaho. It still looks like the \"drier\" spots\nwill be across Lava Beds where 0.50-1.0\" amounts are possible.\nIt also looks like once you get into areas west of US-93 and\nnorth of ID-75, 0.30-0.80\" is possible. We have widened the area\nof where we are more likely to see, and have in the official\nforecast, over 2\"...generally east of a line from Monida Pass to\naround Juniper and Sweetzer Summit. It is likely we will see\nlocalized amounts around 3\" along and east of I-15. Some of the\nwobbling is due to the models showing some uncertainty where \nsome of the heaviest bands end up Sunday into Monday. The trend \nhas been to keep split flow, BUT instead of the main closed low \nbeing offshore or over California...the trend is shifting \nnortheast toward the Sierras to central Utah. Looking at the \nhigher end of the Blend of Models, we are coming in at or just \nabove the 90th percentile mark in terms of heavier amounts. The \nabsolute highest amounts with the Blend are still running in the\n2-4\" range...and 4\"+ in spots across the eastern and southeast\nhighlands. Those amounts would represent a very, very low\nprobability but higher impact situation. Given the pattern and\nthe influences from Rachel, it is possible we see some isolated\nhigh-end amounts. Needless to say, the FLOOD WATCH remains in\nplace. We may need to expand the areal extent into the central\nmountains depending upon trends in the models, or at least\nconsider watches for the Ohio and/or Wapiti Burn Scars. We must\nneglect that colder air is going produce some snow at highest\nelevations of the central mountains and along the Montana\nborder. While we might see a few spots below 8000ft with some\nbrief snow accumulation, that still looks like the lowest we see\ndecent accumulations. We could see over 6\" on the highest peaks,\n9500-10000ft, and certainly once you get north into Montana\nwhere the coldest air will stay. \n\nIt will take until Wednesday or Thursday to see low pressure\nshift east of the Rockies. Lingering showers and cooler\ntemperatures will remain in place through then. High pressure\nshould begin to slowly return later next week. Temperatures will\nslowly climb back into the 60s for highs in the valleys.",
